  "identity_decision": {
    "recommendation": "Retain two narrow algorithm identities.",
    "reason": "The ideal fixed-parameter information objective is the same after consistent min/max conversion, but the papers specify different approximations and operational inference: ES uses finite representer probabilities, EP derivatives and a prospective-loss expansion; PES uses approximate maximizer draws, derivative/EP conditioning and predictive entropy. Mutual-information algebra alone does not collapse these operational methods. No claim that their selected points differ on every problem is necessary.",
    "candidates": [
      {
        "id": "AIMech:EntropySearch",
        "name": "Entropy Search",
        "concept_kind": "algorithm",
        "aliases": [],
        "source_id": "hennig2012",
        "frontier_owner": 176,
        "proposed_scope": "The Hennig–Schuler2012 representer-point, EP and approximate expected-information-gain optimizer, rather than every information-based Bayesian optimization method.",
        "definition_for_assessment": "A Bayesian global optimization algorithm that represents the location of a function minimum on sampled representer points, approximates its probabilities with expectation propagation, and chooses evaluations using an approximate expected reduction in relative-entropy loss.",
        "mechanism": [
          {
            "claim": "Model a latent objective on a bounded continuous domain with a sufficiently regular GP and a specified observation likelihood; the core analytically tractable case is Gaussian observation noise. Infer a law for the minimizer rather than optimizing the posterior mean at every evaluation.",
            "locators": "Sections 1.1 and 2.1, PDF pp.2,5–7; Appendix A p.26."
          },
          {
            "claim": "Sample finite representer points from a proposal with support across the domain, using PI/EI-shaped densities as proposed heuristics. The points are a quadrature representation; the next evaluation need not be one of them. Proposal values correct the nonuniform discrete loss, rather than replacing minimizer probabilities.",
            "locators": "Section 2.2 p.8; Section 2.5 p.13; Algorithm 1 p.19."
          },
          {
            "claim": "For each representer point, use EP to approximate Gaussian inequality probabilities that it is the minimum; normalize/use the resulting finite probability representation and its derivatives with respect to the GP mean/covariance. This is approximate inference, not exact integration of the continuous optimizer law.",
            "locators": "Section 2.3 PDF pp.8–10; Algorithm 1 p.19."
          },
          {
            "claim": "Use negative KL to a fixed uniform base as a loss. On the nonuniform discretization the varying part is H(q)-sum_i q_i log u_i, up to constants. This is more specific than entropy of the categorical weights alone.",
            "locators": "Section 2.5 PDF pp.12–13."
          },
          {
            "claim": "Combine prospective GP mean/covariance innovations with an EP derivative expansion and fixed Monte Carlo innovation draws to approximate expected one-step loss change, then optimize this differentiable acquisition numerically and repeat after observation. The procedure is greedy, not optimal planning over the full remaining horizon.",
            "locators": "Sections 2.4,2.6–2.7 PDF pp.11,13–15; Algorithm 1 p.19."
          },
          {
            "claim": "Hyperparameter learning is considered, with a proposed mixture-to-single-GP approximation, but the decision calculation does not include the full hierarchical learning process. At the horizon Algorithm 1 returns the approximate minimizer belief for a final decision; the experiments separately use a posterior-mean optimum recommendation.",
            "locators": "Section 2.8.2 p.16; Algorithm 1 p.19; Section 3.1 p.20."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "AIMech:PredictiveEntropySearch",
        "name": "Predictive Entropy Search",
        "concept_kind": "algorithm",
        "aliases": [],
        "source_id": "hernandez2014",
        "supplement_source_id": "hernandez2014-supplement",
        "frontier_owner": 177,
        "proposed_scope": "The original PES2014 approximate predictive-entropy acquisition and sequential optimizer. PESC, parallel PES and max-value entropy methods are not automatic aliases or newly assessed leaves.",
        "definition_for_assessment": "A Bayesian global optimization algorithm that approximates information about the global maximizer by subtracting maximizer-conditioned predictive entropy from ordinary predictive entropy, using approximate maximizer samples and Gaussian constraint approximations.",
        "mechanism": [
          {
            "claim": "For fixed hyperparameters the target is information between the optimizer location and a future noisy evaluation. Mutual-information symmetry rewrites the same ideal optimizer-entropy reduction as predictive entropy minus expected predictive entropy conditioned on the optimizer. This is not simply maximizing predictive entropy.",
            "locators": "Main Section 2, PDF pp.2–3, Eqs.2–3."
          },
          {
            "claim": "For a shift-invariant real kernel with a usable spectral distribution, draw random Fourier features and Gaussian posterior weights, optimize each resulting finite-feature sample function, and treat its maximizing location as an approximate posterior maximizer draw. Feature count and numerical maximization remain approximation choices.",
            "locators": "Main Section 2.1 p.3, Eq.5; Supplement A pp.1–2 / printed10–11."
          },
          {
            "claim": "Approximate maximizer conditioning with zero-gradient/selected Hessian information, negative diagonal factors and a soft comparison with the largest noisy observation. Compute a Gaussian approximation using EP. The later sampled off-diagonal substitution does not make negative diagonals a general local-maximum certificate.",
            "locators": "Main Section 2.2 pp.4–5, Eqs.6–7; Supplement B.1–B.2 pp.2–3 / printed11–12."
          },
          {
            "claim": "At candidate x impose f(x)<f(x_star) on a two-variable Gaussian approximation, obtain a truncated-normal variance, then approximate its marginal by a moment-matched Gaussian. Add the observation-noise variance before using the Gaussian entropy. The variance formula needs v=Var(f(x_star)-f(x))>0 and valid covariance; moments do not make the truncated law Gaussian.",
            "locators": "Main p.5, Eqs.8–9; Supplement B.3 pp.3–4 / printed12–13."
          },
          {
            "claim": "Average the approximate variance-log differences over sampled GP hyperparameters and corresponding maximizer draws, with each noise variance belonging to its draw. Candidate-independent conditioning/EP terms can be reused. This is the source conditional-acquisition average, not a general identity for entropy under the fully marginalized model.",
            "locators": "Main Algorithm 2 p.2 and Section 2.3 p.5, Eq.10."
          },
          {
            "claim": "Choose the next noisy evaluation by maximizing the approximate acquisition, update data and repeat. Algorithm 1 returns a maximizer of the final GP posterior mean as its recommendation, which differs from selecting a maximizer-probability mode and is not an optimum certificate.",
            "locators": "Main Section 1 pp.1–2 and Algorithm 1 p.2."
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "direction_convention": "ES is presented as minimization; PES as maximization. Negating the latent objective and observed values with an appropriately transformed prior converts the target direction. Direction alone is not the identity distinction.",
    "aliases": "No acronym alias is needed; ES is especially ambiguous. Generic entropy search, Bayesian optimization, GP regression, EI, Thompson sampling and maximum entropy are not exact aliases."
  },
