    {
      "id": "ES-BOUNDARY-MEASURE",
      "status": "mathematical domain limitation",
      "locator": "ES Figure 2 PDF p.7 / printed p.1815; Section 2.5 pp.12–13 / pp.1820–1821; PES Eqs.2–3 pp.2–3",
      "observation": "ES explicitly recognizes boundary atoms in the optimizer law. A law with atoms is not a Lebesgue density; KL to a purely continuous uniform base is infinite when such atoms have positive mass. A naive differential-entropy subtraction then need not be well-defined.",
      "handling": "Use the finite discretized ES loss with its stated approximation, or qualify ideal density/finite-entropy formulas. General mutual information is a measure-theoretic KL and need not require a Lebesgue optimizer density; this does not by itself validate a particular numerical approximation."
    },
    {
      "id": "PES-BOUNDARY-STATIONARITY",
      "status": "mathematical domain limitation",
      "locator": "PES main PDF pp.1,3–4, bounded domain and C1",
      "observation": "A bounded-domain maximum can occur at the boundary with nonzero gradient, for example f(x)=x on [0,1]. Zero-gradient and negative-Hessian constraints describe an interior stationary approximation, not all bounded-domain maxima.",
      "handling": "Retain this limitation and require an explicit boundary treatment before implementing those constraints universally."
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-INNOVATION-TAYLOR-NOTATION",
      "status": "unresolved implementation-level audit, not a completed correction",
      "locator": "ES Section 2.4 PDF p.11 and Eq.6 PDF p.13",
      "observation": "The displayed covariance innovation is positive while called an increment; the noise placement and second-mean-derivative coefficient also need a consistent signed-increment/Taylor interpretation before literal implementation.",
      "handling": "The supported high-level method is GP innovation plus a first-order EP-based expected-loss approximation. This task neither implements nor claims to have repaired these printed formulas. Exact Gaussian conditioning must contract covariance at fixed hyperparameters; a full formula-level audit remains separate work."
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-NONCORE-LOG-CONTEXT",
      "status": "confirmed context caveats outside the proposed core",
      "locator": "ES Section 2.8.3 PDF p.17 / printed p.1825",
      "observation": "The text calls logarithm convex, and the displayed log-likelihood second derivative at g=log(y) has positive sign. Log is concave but increasing; for the stated Gaussian likelihood the second derivative is -y^2/sigma^2.",
