      "handling": "Use the posterior condition. The independent-grid maximum then stochastically dominates under the stated comparison; this is not a pathwise upper bound for each independently generated sample."
    },
    {
      "id": "MES-GUMBEL-FIT",
      "status": "approximation and guard boundary",
      "locator": "Main PDF p.3 Section3.1/Algorithm1; supplement PDF pp.1–2 Section2",
      "observation": "Independent finite, differently distributed Gaussian maxima are not generally exactly Gumbel. Percentile fitting is another approximation and can cross the target CDF, even for a singleton.",
      "handling": "Do not transfer a preceding stochastic-order guarantee automatically through this fit. The inverse-CDF computation needs 0<r<1 and a positive scale for finite real outputs; zero-variance/degenerate quantiles need their own policy."
    },
    {
      "id": "MES-RFF-NORMALIZATION",
      "status": "source formula qualification",
      "locator": "Main PDF p.4 Section3.2",
      "observation": "The Fourier transform of a stationary covariance is a finite nonnegative spectral measure with total mass k(0), not a probability measure without normalization. The displayed sqrt(2/D) cosine scaling assumes unit amplitude.",
      "handling": "Use a normalized spectral distribution and the appropriate kernel-amplitude factor. The random-feature and numerical maximum approximations do not produce exact original-process maximum draws."
    },
    {
      "id": "MES-SINGLE-SAMPLE-UCB",
      "status": "parameter-domain qualification",
      "locator": "Main PDF p.4 Lemma3.1",
      "observation": "The one-sample score is a decreasing function of the standardized gap, giving the stated PI/EST selection relation under matching parameters and positive posterior standard deviations. However the displayed square-root UCB parameter min_x gamma can be negative for some sampled thresholds.",
